  [BUG] firmware/fw_base.S:62-64  _try_lottery
      Boot hart lottery emits amoswap.w when __riscv_zaamo is defined. AMOSwap is NOT in the Ziccamoa (AMOArithmetic) subset. This is the very first instruction executed — before any trap handler (mtvec) is installed. On Ziccamoa-only hardware where amoswap traps on main memory, this causes an immediate, unrecoverable machine-mode exception with no recovery path. The result is a silent boot hang.
  [BUG] lib/sbi/riscv_atomic.c:78-103  __axchg
      __axchg macro emits amoswap.w.aqrl / amoswap.d.aqrl when __riscv_zaamo is defined. AMOSwap is NOT in the Ziccamoa (AMOArithmetic) subset. This is the most commonly called atomic swap path (via atomic_xchg, atomic_raw_xchg_uint, atomic_raw_xchg_ulong). On Ziccamoa-only hardware, every call site traps. The codebase conflates Zaamo detection with full AMO support — Ziccamoa only guarantees AMOADD, AMOAND, AMOOR, AMOXOR, AMOMIN/MAX/MINU/MAXU.
  [BUG] lib/sbi/sbi_illegal_atomic.c:18-23  sbi_illegal_atomic
      When __riscv_zaamo is defined, ALL trapped AMO opcodes are unconditionally routed to truly_illegal_insn with no emulation fallback. On Ziccamoa-only hardware where unsupported AMO instructions (amoswap, LR/SC) trap on main memory, the handler returns sbi_trap_redirect with a fatal error instead of emulating them. This turns recoverable traps into fatal errors. The __riscv_zalrsc path (lines 25-661) contains a complete AMO emulation layer using LR/SC sequences, but it is entirely dead code under the Zaamo compile path.
  [BUG] lib/sbi/riscv_locks.c:28-43  spin_trylock
      spin_trylock uses lr.w.aq / sc.w.rl UNCONDITIONALLY — there is NO compile-time guard checking for Zaamo, Zalrsc, or __riscv_atomic at all. LR/SC are NOT in the Ziccamoa (AMOArithmetic) subset. On Ziccamoa-only hardware, any call to spin_trylock would trap on main memory. This is the only location found exclusively by a slow path analysis that was missed by entity-index approaches because there's no #ifdef to index on. This represents a direct crash risk in lock acquisition paths throughout the codebase.
  [BUG] lib/sbi/sbi_illegal_atomic.c:622-660  amo_insn_table / sbi_illegal_atomic
      The full AMO emulation dispatch table (amo_insn_table, lines 622-655) and the sbi_illegal_atomic entry point that indexes into it (line 657-660) are compiled ONLY under __riscv_zalrsc. Under __riscv_zaamo, this entire emulation infrastructure is dead code. On Ziccamoa hardware where amoswap and LR/SC trap, there is no mechanism to emulate unsupported AMOs using AMOArithmetic-safe sequences. The #ifdef architecture creates a false dichotomy: either you have full hardware AMO support (Zaamo) or you have no hardware AMOs and emulate everything via LR/SC (Zalrsc). Ziccamoa (partial hardware AMO support) falls through the crack.
  [RISK] Makefile:317-326  (ISA default)
      Default PLATFORM_RISCV_ISA is 'rv<XLEN>imafdc' which includes the full 'A' extension, causing __riscv_atomic to be defined. This enables ALL AMO operations (including amoswap and LR/SC) as native instructions. There is no build-time knob to select a Ziccamoa-aware configuration that would restrict codegen to AMOArithmetic-safe operations only. This is the root-cause infrastructure gap: the build system cannot distinguish between 'A' (full atomic), 'Zaamo' (all AMOs including swap), and 'Ziccamoa' (AMOArithmetic only).
  [RISK] include/sbi/sbi_hart.h:29-93  sbi_hart_extensions
      The runtime ISA extension bitmap enum (sbi_hart_extensions) has no entries for ZICCAMOA, ZAAMO, or ZALRSC. The FDT parser has no mechanism to detect or record Ziccamoa presence at runtime, making it impossible to dynamically adjust atomic operation strategy (e.g., emulating AMOSwap with AMOArithmetic sequences when only Ziccamoa is present). Any future runtime Ziccamoa detection code has no infrastructure to store the result.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_hart.c:368-399  sbi_hart_ext
      The name-to-bitmap lookup table (sbi_hart_ext[]) lacks zaamo, zalrsc, and ziccamoa entries. FDT-parsed ISA extension strings containing these names are silently ignored by both the legacy riscv,isa parser and the riscv,isa-extensions parser. This means that even if a DTB correctly declares Ziccamoa, OpenSBI has no way to record that information and adapt its atomic-operation strategy.
  [RISK] lib/utils/fdt/fdt_helper.c:333-398  fdt_parse_isa_one_hart
      Parses legacy riscv,isa strings by iterating sbi_hart_ext[] (line 390), which lacks zaamo/ziccamoa entries. Multi-letter extensions not in the table are silently skipped (lines 384-393). If the FDT declares 'rv64imafdc_ziccamoa' in the riscv,isa property, the ziccamoa extension string is parsed but discarded because no matching entry exists. Ziccamoa declared in FDT is completely invisible to OpenSBI at runtime.
  [RISK] lib/utils/fdt/fdt_helper.c:400-410  fdt_parse_isa_extensions_one_hart
      Parses riscv,isa-extensions string list against sbi_hart_ext[] (line 406-408). Same gap as fdt_parse_isa_one_hart: ziccamoa and zaamo are not recognized. The lookup iterates only known entries; unrecognized extension names in the FDT string list are silently ignored. Both FDT parsing paths therefore silently discard Ziccamoa and Zaamo declarations.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/riscv_locks.c:48-65  spin_lock
      spin_lock uses amoadd.w.aqrl when __riscv_zaamo is defined. AMOADD IS in the Ziccamoa (AMOArithmetic) subset, so this specific instruction is currently safe on Ziccamoa hardware. The concern is the #ifdef conflation: __riscv_zaamo detection is treated as equivalent to full AMO support, with no mechanism to restrict to AMOArithmetic-only operations. If any non-AMOArithmetic instruction (e.g., amoswap, LR/SC) were added behind this same guard, it would break on Ziccamoa hardware. The invariant 'only AMOArithmetic instructions are placed under this guard' is not enforced in code.
