PR #2913  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: Ziccamoa clarified to require only AMOArithmetic-level (not full Zaamo) PMA support; software assuming full Zaamo atomics from Ziccamoa presence may use unsupported operations
  [BUG] sys/riscv/riscv/identcpu.c:87-104  cpu_desc (struct)
      cpu_desc has no fields for atomic sub-extension tracking. The z_extensions bitmask (u_int, line 98) defines only Z_ZICBOM (1<<0), Z_ZICBOZ (1<<1), Z_ZICBOP (1<<2). No bits exist for Ziccamoa, Zaamo, or Zalasr. The isa_extensions field (line 90) is a single u_int populated by HWCAP_ISA_BIT() for single-letter 'a', providing only monolithic A-extension presence/absence. Atomic sub-extensions are structurally invisible to the entire detection framework — the kernel literally cannot record their state even if it wanted to.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/riscv/identcpu.c:261-333  parse_riscv_isa
      Directly creates the over-assumption. Parses single-letter 'a' (line 275) into desc->isa_extensions |= HWCAP_ISA_BIT(isa[i]) unconditionally, treating the entire A-extension (LR/SC, AMOSwap, AMOArithmetic) as a monolithic capability. The switch statement's 'z' case (line 314-319) delegates to parse_ext_z(), but parse_ext_z() only recognizes zicbom/zicboz/zicbop. Multi-letter extensions 'ziccamoa', 'zaamo', and 'zalasr' — which the RISC-V ISA string spec defines to represent these sub-capabilities — begin with 'z', enter parse_ext_z(), fail all three CHECK_Z_EXT macros, and are silently consumed by the while-loop fallthrough (lines 231-233 in parse_ext_z). Their presence is undetectable, their absence is indistinguishable from presence.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/riscv/identcpu.c:209-236  parse_ext_z
      Z-extension parser only recognizes zicbom/zicboz/zicbop via CHECK_Z_EXT macros. Multi-letter extensions like 'ziccamoa', 'zaamo', 'zalasr' — all starting with 'z' — enter this function but fail every CHECK_Z_EXT match. They are then silently consumed by the fallthrough while-loop (lines 231-233) which skips to the next '_' separator or string end. The kernel has zero visibility into whether these extensions were present in the ISA string. This is the direct mechanism of the over-assumption: hardware declaring Ziccamoa but not full Zaamo would produce an ISA string containing 'ziccamoa' that is parsed and discarded with no capability bit set.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/riscv/identcpu.c:444-476  update_global_capabilities
      Propagates the monolithic isa_extensions bitmask (containing HWCAP_ISA_A) to global elf_hwcap via UPDATE_CAP at line 457, using AND-reduction across all CPUs. No sub-extension granularity for atomic capabilities exists. The only Z-extension bits propagated are zicbom/zicboz/zicbop (lines 471-473). This means if even one CPU in a heterogeneous system lacks full Zaamo (but has Ziccamoa), the kernel still cannot detect or communicate this distinction — elf_hwcap simply carries a monolithic 'A' bit or not.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/include/riscvreg.h:52-54  (top-level defines)
      SCAUSE exception code definitions skip from code 13 (SCAUSE_LOAD_PAGE_FAULT) directly to code 15 (SCAUSE_STORE_PAGE_FAULT). Code 14 — SCAUSE_STORE_AMO_PAGE_FAULT, defined by the RISC-V privileged spec v1.11+ specifically for AMO operations that fault — is omitted entirely. The kernel cannot distinguish an AMO page fault from load or store page faults at the exception dispatch level. No #define exists anywhere in the riscv tree for this exception code.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/riscv/trap.c:306-377  do_trap_supervisor
      Switch statement (lines 333-376) handles LOAD_PAGE_FAULT and STORE_PAGE_FAULT (line 348-350) but has no case for exception code 14 (AMO page fault). Any AMO page fault in supervisor mode — which would occur if Ziccamoa-only hardware raises a fault for unsupported LR/SC or AMOSwap in main memory — falls through to the 'default' case (line 372), which unconditionally panics with 'Unknown kernel exception'. This is a kernel crash (denial of service) on any AMO fault, not just a misclassification.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/riscv/trap.c:379-460  do_trap_user
      Same gap in user-mode trap dispatch. Switch statement (lines 410-459) handles STORE_PAGE_FAULT and LOAD_PAGE_FAULT (lines 425-427) but has no case for AMO page fault (code 14). Falls through to 'default' (line 455) which panics with 'Unknown userland exception'. A user-space process executing atomic operations (LR/SC or AMOSwap) on memory lacking PMA support under Ziccamoa-only hardware would trigger an AMO page fault, causing a kernel panic — a local unprivileged user can crash the system.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/riscv/trap.c:256-262  page_fault_handler
      Fault type dispatch only checks SCAUSE_STORE_PAGE_FAULT (maps to VM_PROT_WRITE) and SCAUSE_INST_PAGE_FAULT (maps to VM_PROT_EXECUTE), falling through to VM_PROT_READ for everything else. An AMO page fault (code 14) would be misclassified as a plain read fault, despite the RISC-V spec requiring that AMO operations need both read AND write permission on the target page. The vm_fault_trap call at line 272 would be invoked with VM_PROT_READ only, potentially succeeding on a read-only mapping where an AMO should fault. This is a silent correctness bug: AMOs could succeed on pages where they should trap.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/include/atomic.h:192-213  atomic_cmpset_32
      Unconditionally emits LR.W/SC.W (load-reserved/store-conditional). LR/SC are Zaamo instructions — NOT guaranteed by Ziccamoa, which only mandates AMOArithmetic (amoadd/amoand/amoor/amoxor/amomin/max) PMA support. On hardware declaring 'ziccamoa' in the ISA string but lacking full Zaamo PMA support for LR/SC in main memory, the LR.W at line 203 or SC.W at line 205 would raise a store/AMO access fault or AMO page fault. This is representative of the LR/SC over-assumption pattern affecting all LR/SC-using atomics.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/include/atomic.h:391-412  atomic_cmpset_64
      Unconditionally emits LR.D/SC.D. Double-width LR/SC fall under Zaamo, not Ziccamoa (AMOArithmetic-only). Same over-assumption as the 32-bit counterpart. On Ziccamoa-only hardware, these 64-bit LR/SC operations on main memory may fault.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/include/atomic.h:215-238  atomic_fcmpset_32
      Unconditionally uses LR.W/SC.W in the fcmpset loop (lines 226, 228). These are Zaamo instructions not guaranteed by Ziccamoa. Same LR/SC over-assumption pattern.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/include/atomic.h:414-437  atomic_fcmpset_64
      Unconditionally uses LR.D/SC.D (lines 425, 427). Another LR/SC pair that Ziccamoa (AMOArithmetic-only) does not mandate PMA support for.
  [BUG] sys/riscv/include/atomic.h:253-267  atomic_readandclear_32
